<p class="lead">You run a plumbing, HVAC, roofing, or electrical business. Your website loads in 4.2 seconds on mobile. A homeowner searches "emergency plumber near me" at 11 PM, clicks your link, and stares at a blank screen while water floods their basement. They hit back. They call your competitor. This scenario plays out thousands of times daily across the home services industry. Website speed has a direct, measurable relationship with how many qualified leads a contractor generates, and it's one Google has documented in its own research for years. In this guide, you'll learn the speed benchmarks that matter for contractor websites, why slow load times cost you the emergency jobs that pay the best, and the specific technical fixes that move the needle on Core Web Vitals and lead volume.</p>

<h2>The Short Version: Why Speed Drives (or Kills) Contractor Leads</h2>

<p>Home service websites live or die on a narrow window of patience. A homeowner with a burst pipe, a dead air conditioner in July, or a leaking roof mid-storm is not browsing casually — they're searching under pressure, ready to hire the first competent-looking contractor who responds. If your site takes too long to show them a phone number, they're already calling someone else.</p>

<p>The pattern is well documented and consistent across the web: sites that load quickly retain more visitors, convert a higher share of those visitors into calls and form submissions, and rank better in local search. Sites that load slowly lose visitors before those visitors ever see a phone number or a contact form. For contractors, that isn't an abstract UX concern — it's leads and revenue walking out the door before the page even finishes rendering.</p>

<p>A useful way to think about where your site sits is against Google's own performance tiers, based on mobile Lighthouse Performance score:</p>

